[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The decisions this architecture rests on, each with the requirement that drove
it and the cost it carries.

These were a markdown table in `architecture.md`. They are here instead because
they are the part of the architecture that has identity: 21 stable IDs, cited
over a hundred times from the firmware, each answering to a requirement. A
`Satisfies` relation to that requirement is checked when this document is
built, so a decision can no longer cite a requirement that does not exist, and
the requirements document can show what implements each of its clauses.

The rest of the architecture -- the component diagrams, the task and timing
tables, the memory budgets, the log record layout -- stays in
[`architecture.md`](architecture.md). None of it has an ID, so there is nothing
here for a tool to check, and a diagram is better read rendered than quoted.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: AD-01
STATEMENT: >>>
**Ports and adapters** (hexagonal). A `kiln_core` component holds all decision logic; hardware is reached only through interface headers in `kiln_ports`; adapters in `kiln_hal_esp32s3` implement them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Directly realises [LINK: TR-01], [LINK: TR-02]. Logic becomes ordinary C that a host compiler builds and a debugger steps through in microseconds.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
One indirection on every hardware access; interfaces must be designed, not grown.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-01
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-02
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-02
STATEMENT: >>>
**Time is injected**, never read. Core functions receive `dt` or take a `kiln_clock_t*`.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: TR-03]. A 168 h firing, a 15 min runaway timer and a 2 h autotune are all testable in milliseconds of wall time.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Every core API grows a time parameter; discipline needed to keep `esp_timer_get_time()` out of the core.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-03
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-03
STATEMENT: >>>
**Explicit context structs, no globals** in the core.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: TR-04]. Lets one test process run several controllers, and makes every test independent.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Slightly more verbose call sites.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-04
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-04
STATEMENT: >>>
**The safety supervisor is a separate, higher-priority task that owns the heat-enable output.** The control path can only *request* heat; safety alone grants it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: SR-01], [LINK: SR-02], [LINK: SR-13]. A bug, deadlock or overrun anywhere in control, HMI, web or networking cannot produce heat.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Two components must agree on a shared view of the plant; that view is passed as an immutable snapshot, not shared state.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-01
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-02
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-13
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-05
STATEMENT: >>>
**Heat enable is a software-generated square wave into a hardware charge pump**, not a static level.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: SR-02], [LINK: HR-07], and gives [LINK: SR-14] for free: a hung safety task stops toggling and the contactor drops in ~1 s with no code involved.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Requires the RC/charge-pump circuit on the board; the toggle must never be delegated to a hardware PWM peripheral, or the property is lost.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-02
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HR-07
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-14
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-06
STATEMENT: >>>
**The setpoint generator is separate from the PID.** The program produces a moving setpoint; the PID only ever tracks the setpoint it is given.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-CTL-09]. The two hardest pieces of behaviour, curve execution with hold-back, and loop tuning, become independently testable. Also makes manual mode and autotune trivially reuse the same PID.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
An extra component and an extra data hand-off per cycle.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-CTL-09
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-07
STATEMENT: >>>
**The SSR window is driven by a 10 ms periodic timer callback** that reads a duty value published atomically by the control task.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-CTL-07], [LINK: FR-CTL-08], [LINK: NFR-04]. Switching precision is decoupled from the 1 s control period and from any task scheduling delay.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The callback runs in a timer context: it must be allocation-free, lock-free and short.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-CTL-07
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-CTL-08
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-04
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-17
STATEMENT: >>>
**Heater current is measured gated to the commanded output state**, not continuously averaged: conduction current is sampled inside a commanded-on interval and leakage current inside a commanded-off interval, each after a settle delay.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-CUR-04]. A time-proportional output at 2 % duty is off 98 % of the time; a blind average would read ~2 % of full current and make [LINK: SR-25]/[LINK: SR-26] meaningless. Gating is what turns the measurement into a statement about the *relay*.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The sampler must know the window phase, so it is driven by the same 10 ms timer that owns the SSR pin ([LINK: AD-07]), not by an independent ADC loop. Windows too short to measure are skipped rather than mis-measured.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-CUR-04
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-25
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SR-26
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-18
STATEMENT: >>>
**The log record grows from 16 to 20 bytes** to carry current and its validity flags.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-CUR-09]. Current without a flag saying whether the sample was a conduction, leakage or skipped measurement is uninterpretable.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
204 records per sector instead of 255; capacity falls from 362 h to **290 h** at the default interval, still well above the 150 h of [LINK: FR-LOG-07]. Recomputed in [§10.4](#104-flash-endurance-analysis).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-CUR-09
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-08
STATEMENT: >>>
**Sample logs live in a dedicated raw flash partition as a circular array of fixed 16-byte records**, not in a filesystem.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-LOG-05]–[LINK: FR-LOG-08], [LINK: CON-03]. A filesystem adds metadata writes, fragmentation, and a torn-write failure mode across structures we do not control. A ring of fixed records has a trivially provable wear pattern ([§10.4](#104-flash-endurance-analysis)) and a reader that cannot be confused by a power cut.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
A bespoke store to implement and test; no `ls` over logs. Mitigated by `tools/logdump`.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-LOG-05
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-LOG-08
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: CON-03
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-09
STATEMENT: >>>
**The log is also the power-loss journal.** Recovery state is reconstructed from the log tail rather than from a separate periodic write.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-RUN-08], [LINK: FR-RUN-09], [LINK: NFR-14]. Removes a 60 000-write-per-run NVS hot spot; the data was already being written.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Recovery granularity equals the log sample interval (default 10 s), which is well inside the [LINK: FR-RUN-08] tolerance.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-RUN-08
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-RUN-09
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-14
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-10
STATEMENT: >>>
**Configuration in NVS; programs and run records in a raw partition of fixed slots.** Originally LittleFS, revised by [LINK: AD-21].
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Config is small, typed, and benefits from NVS wear levelling. Programs and run records want names and atomic replace, which is what the slot store provides; they do not want a directory, because nothing addresses them by anything but a slot number.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Two storage mechanisms to initialise. No general filesystem on the device, so there is no path for a file that is not a program or a run record.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: AD-11
STATEMENT: >>>
**Web assets are gzipped and embedded in the application image**, not stored in a filesystem.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-WEB-02], [LINK: FR-UPD-05]. Assets and firmware version can never disagree, and an OTA update is atomic across both.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Asset size counts against the 2 MB OTA partition; budget in [§12.4](#124-asset-budget).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-WEB-02
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-UPD-05
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-12
STATEMENT: >>>
**Live updates by Server-Sent Events**, not WebSocket.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-WEB-05]. Telemetry is one-directional; SSE is plain HTTP, needs no framing layer, reconnects by itself, and costs one socket. Commands go over ordinary `POST`.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
No client→server push channel; not needed, since no command is latency-critical.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-WEB-05
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-13
STATEMENT: >>>
**Tasks communicate by queues and immutable snapshots**, with no mutex on the control path.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: NFR-01], [LINK: NFR-02]. Removes priority inversion and unbounded blocking as a class of failure.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Snapshot copying each cycle; at ~64 bytes per snapshot this is negligible.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-01
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-02
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-14
STATEMENT: >>>
**Dual-target build**: the same core sources compile as ESP-IDF components and as a plain CMake library for host tests.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: TR-01], [LINK: TR-13], [LINK: TR-19]. Coverage and sanitisers are available where the logic actually lives.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The core may not include a single IDF header; enforced by a build check ([§14.5](#145-enforcement)).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-01
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-13
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-19
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-15
STATEMENT: >>>
**Core affinity split**: connectivity and UI on core 0, control and safety on core 1.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: NFR-01], [LINK: NFR-02]. WiFi (priority 23) and lwIP cannot preempt the control or safety task at all, so their deadlines do not depend on radio behaviour.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The core is tied to a dual-core target; [LINK: CON-02] fixes ESP32-S3, which is dual-core.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-01
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-02
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-19
STATEMENT: >>>
**The log ring is `kiln_core/logring` over a `port_flash`**, not logic inside the log-store adapter.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Everything interesting about the ring is a decision with a failure mode [LINK: FR-LOG-06] and [LINK: FR-LOG-08] name explicitly: head discovery across 512 sector headers, erase-immediately-before-write ordering so a power cut cannot destroy data the index still claims, and terminating a sector scan at a torn record. Behind `esp_partition` none of it is testable; in the core it is driven by a fake that can cut power mid-write. Revises [§5.2](#52-ports)'s original placement.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
One more port, and the core now provides an implementation of `port_logstore` rather than only consuming ports. The adapter in exchange has no logic left to get wrong, three calls that pass straight through.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-LOG-06
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-LOG-08
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-20
STATEMENT: >>>
**The firmware is C++20, analysed by `clang-tidy` as a high-integrity profile.** Not MISRA: `clang-tidy` implements no MISRA checks, and the `hicpp-*` module that approximated High Integrity C++ has been removed from LLVM. The equivalent content is enabled under its current names (`cppcoreguidelines-*`, `bugprone-*`, `cert-*`, `clang-analyzer-*`).
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: CON-05] permits C or C++; [LINK: NFR-25] requires a static analysis configuration and [LINK: TR-24] requires CI to run it, and the C toolchain offered no comparable check set. The migration cost was 9 compile errors across 22 597 lines, because the code already had no heap, no VLAs and explicit context structs. C++20 rather than C++17 because the 152 designated initialisers that name every safety default are a C99 feature C++ regained only in C++20.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
A `-std=gnu++20` floor on both build halves. Genuine MISRA C++ compliance is now a *commercial tooling* decision rather than a language one, and nothing in the repository may claim it. Three check groups are deferred rather than clean, `tasklist.md` section F.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: CON-05
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: NFR-25
  ROLE: Satisfies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TR-24
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-21
STATEMENT: >>>
**`port_filestore` is `kiln_core/fileslots` over a `port_flash`, a fixed array of two-sector regions, not a filesystem.** Revises [LINK: AD-10].
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Two reasons, and either alone would be enough. The first is [LINK: CON-04]: LittleFS is not in the ESP-IDF tree, so it arrives either as a managed dependency, which [LINK: CON-04] forbids at build time, or as several thousand vendored lines to be maintained and licence-audited. The second is that nothing above it wants a filesystem. `program_store` and `run_index` address `/p/00` to `/p/19` and `/r/00` upward, and between them call read, write_atomic, remove and usage; `list` and `exists` have no caller at all. That is an array. [LINK: AD-08] already refused a filesystem for the log, on the grounds that it adds metadata writes, fragmentation and a torn-write mode across structures we do not control, and the same reasoning survives being applied to a second fixed-size store. The in-tree alternatives were considered and rejected: SPIFFS and FAT are both reachable without a fetch and neither is power-fail safe, which is the one property this store is required to have.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
One file occupies one region of two erase sectors and alternates between them, so the copy being replaced is never the copy being erased. A name maps to a region through an index built at mount, 64 entries of 72 bytes. The cost is capacity: 40 files in 512 kB where a filesystem would hold hundreds of small ones, which is irrelevant at a fixed 20 programs and 20 run records and would not be if that ever stopped being fixed. See [§10.6](#106-the-file-store).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: CON-04
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-16
STATEMENT: >>>
**The web UI is served only through the public REST API** it documents.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: FR-WEB-19]. The API is exercised by the UI on every use, so it cannot rot; and the UI is replaceable.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
No shortcuts for the UI; occasionally a slightly chattier interaction.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: FR-WEB-19
  ROLE: Satisfies

[DECISION]
UID: AD-22
STATEMENT: >>>
**The absolute over-temperature trip, the thermocouple-fault trip and the lid
trip move to a second microcontroller**, an STM32, which owns the chamber
front end and one series element in the contactor coil. It reports temperature
and status to the ESP32 over a point-to-point serial link and takes no
instruction from it. Every other function stays in the ESP32.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
`SR-09`, `SR-04` and `SR-31`, and the independence claim of
[`safety.md`](safety.md) section 6, which currently has to admit that L1 to L4
all share one MCU and that the only fully independent layer is `HR-13`, the
external cutout, which is outside this product.

This is the first protection layer inside the product boundary that a defect in
the ESP32 firmware cannot reach, including a defect in the safety supervisor
task of [LINK: AD-04]. It also fixes what tasklist `K1` records about the
discrete fault chain: an open-drain output is closed when the front end is
absent, and the MAX31856 reports no open circuit until its fault mask is
configured, so that interlock is inert out of reset. A supervisor starts
de-energised and permits heat only after it has valid conversions, which turns
absence of evidence into a trip rather than a permission.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
A second firmware to build, review and flash, and the independence is only
worth what that firmware's simplicity is worth: a scheduler, a parser or a
configuration model in it would start to rot the claim.

The ESP32's process value now arrives over the link, so the link is
availability-critical even though it is not protection-critical: silence means
no temperature, which means no heat. The chamber thermocouple remains shared,
so the independence won is against software and MCU failure and not against a
sensor that reads plausibly but wrongly.

Supersedes `HR-24` and removes the discrete chain of tasklist section K
(`Q5`, `Q6`, `R28` to `R31`). The lid's series contact is kept, being the one
element that depends on no firmware at all. Forces `SR-23`'s 1350 degC
configurable ceiling to move, because a hard-coded backstop needs room above
the highest configurable limit.

Designed in [`safety-supervisor.md`](safety-supervisor.md), which carries the
open questions. Does not replace `HR-13`.
<<<
